now that we know the basics of tax  brackets let's think a little bit about  what tax deductions are tax deductions  you will often hear that in the u.s. at  least your mortgage interest is tax  deductible or you might get a tax  deduction for doing something that the  government wants you to do or who knows  what it might be but what I want to do  in this video is just focus on how does  that tax deduction translate into a  reduction in of your taxes so let's say  in the last video we had someone who  made one hundred thousand dollars in  income and we saw that that had them  paying based on this very simple tax  code that I set up that had them paying  twenty three thousand dollars in taxes  now let's say that that person had paid  ten thousand dollars in ten thousand  dollars in mortgage interest and  currently that is a tax deduction in the  u.s. so $10,000 they have a ten thousand  dollar tax deduction now my question to  you is if they have this deduction how  much are they going to pay in taxes well  when you just look at the word tax  deduction he's like oh maybe I just  deduct this value from the taxes so if  they were gonna pay twenty three  thousand maybe I just subtract ten  thousand from that and they're gonna pay  thirteen thousand dollars  that is not how what a tax deduction is  what a tax deduction is it really should  be called an income deduction because  what it does is you then deduct the ten  thousand dollars from your income to say  that instead of making a hundred  thousand dollars you made ninety  thousand dollars and then based on this  lowered income this this income that was  reduced by the tax deduction you then  calculate your taxes so in this scenario  how much would the person pay well  they'll pay zero percent on the first  ten thousand that's still true they'll  still pay 20 percent on the next forty  thousand that's still true and then  they're gonna pay thirty percent not on  fifty thousand the fifty thousand that's  above this threshold they're gonna pay  thirty percent on the forty thousand  that is above the threshold so this is  thirty percent of forty thousand because  their income has been reduced by the  deduction at least for tax purposes and  so thirty percent of forty thousand  dollars well that's twelve thousand  dollars that is twelve thousand dollars  and so their total taxes instead of  being twenty three thousand are gonna be  eight thousand plus twelve thousand  or $20,000 in taxes another way to think  about it is this person and $100,000  their highest tax bracket was in the 30%  and that deduction was the even with the  deduction they stayed in that 30%  bracket they went from $100,000 of  income to $90,000 of income so their  income went down by $10,000 their income  went down by $10,000 they had to pay  they had to pay 30% of that 10,000 less  in taxes so their taxes went down by 3  $3,000 so I just want to reinforce a tax  deduction is not a direct deduction from  your taxes it is a deduction from your  income for tax purposes so you don't  save through 10,000 dollars in taxes you  save $3,000 in taxes in this scenario  now what are their scenarios where you  can deduct directly from your taxes yes  they are there are but they're not  called tax deductions those are called  tax credits so if someone gets a tax  credit let's say that this person right  over here above and beyond this tax  deduction they get a tax credit of  $1,000 then this can be directly this  can be directly taken out of the taxes  so the person with the 10,000 the person  making a hundred thousand with a ten  thousand dollar deduction on a one  thousand dollar credit they will then  pay from the deduction we got them to 20  thousand dollars and then because of the  credit we can subtract out another 1000  and then they will pay 19 thousand  dollars in taxes
 

